    "summary": "High‑energy Frost ’82 show with monster Throwing Stones>Touch and soaring Morning Dew",
    "review": "The October 9, 1982 concert at Palo Alto’s Frost Amphitheatre is a straight‑up firecracker from the early‑’80s era. The band rolls out a tight first set that builds momentum through an inspired “On The Road Again → Beat It On Down The Line” segue and a punchy “West L.A. Fadeaway.” By the time they hit Set 2, the chemistry is unmistakable – a blistering “Throwing Stones > Touch of Grey” launches into a soaring “Estimated Prophet → Eyes of the World,” and the second‑set jam train (Drums → Space → Truckin' → The Other One → Morning Dew) feels like a single, uninterrupted improvisational wave. Jerry’s guitar soars with pure, rotopan tone, Phil’s bass fires on every beat, Brent’s electric piano adds color without ever getting lost, and Bill & Mickey drive the groove with primal power. Even when a few mixes bury Bob’s rhythm work or flatten Brent’s early‑80s piano, the overall vibe stays locked in, culminating in a rousing “U.S. Blues” encore that leaves the crowd buzzing.",
